  urldate = {2026-05-22},
  abstract = {Summary                            The interleukin (               IL               )‐36 cytokines include 3 agonists,               IL               ‐36α,               IL               ‐36β, and               IL               ‐36γ that bind to a common receptor composed of               IL               ‐36R and               IL               ‐1               RA               cP to stimulate inflammatory responses.               IL               ‐36Ra is a natural antagonist that binds to               IL               ‐36R, but does not recruit the co‐receptor               IL               ‐1               RA               cP and does not stimulate any intracellular responses. The               IL               ‐36 cytokines are expressed predominantly by epithelial cells and act on a number of cells including immune cells, epithelial cells, and fibroblasts. Processing of the N‐terminus is required for full agonist or antagonist activity for all               IL               ‐36 members. The role of               IL               ‐36 has been extensively demonstrated in the skin where it can act on keratinocytes and immune cells to induce a robust inflammatory response that has been implicated in psoriatic disorders. Emerging data also suggest a role for this cytokine family in pulmonary and intestinal physiology and pathology.},
  langid = {english},
