  urldate = {2026-05-22},
  abstract = {Mycobacterium scrofulaceum                 is an environmental mycobacterial species rarely reported to cause disseminated infection in adults. We report the case of a disseminated                 M. scrofulaceum                 infection in a 55-year-old nonhuman immunodeficiency virus-infected Thai man with anti-interferon-γ autoantibodies. The clinical signs of the infection improved after the induction regimen with amikacin, rifampicin, ethambutol, and clarithromycin, followed by the consolidation regimen with ethambutol, clarithromycin, and trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole. Our review of previous reported cases of this infection indicates its association with immune deficiency, complex treatment, and a high rate of unfavorable outcomes.},
